#: ../../templates.rst:397
msgid "Modular Typst templates"
msgstr ""

#: ../../templates.rst:400
msgid "The default book, discussion, and article layouts share ``theme.typ``. It defines ``gd-palette``, the font defaults and ``configure-document(body, ...)``. The wrapper applies its rules to the content it receives. The default palette uses Guidedog teal (``#126d78``), dark ink and light neutral panels. Books use a centered cover and teal chapter openings. Discussion records use metadata panels, state badges, a status note and compact contents. Standalone articles use a masthead with authors, publication date, abstract block, and article headings without chapter counters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../templates.rst:402
msgid "``guidedog quickstart`` copies ``theme.typ``, ``book.typ``, ``discussion.typ`` and ``article.typ``. ``guidedog dsc init`` copies the shared theme and discussion layout. All four files are embedded in the executable. Without a project layout, the builder composes a self-contained built-in template; it does not read a project theme. Untrusted builds use that same isolated fallback."
msgstr ""

#: ../../templates.rst:404
msgid "We can edit ``theme.typ`` to change all three copied layouts together. Changes to files beneath ``_templates/``, including nested imported modules and assets, invalidate cached PDFs. The builder tracks directory membership as well, so adding or removing a module cannot leave a stale PDF. Template discovery stays inside the project's readable roots and permits at most 16,384 template inputs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../templates.rst:406
msgid "To adopt a new default in an older project, we first keep a backup of our custom files. We can remove the copied layout to use the embedded version, or copy fresh files from a temporary ``guidedog quickstart`` project. We keep ``theme.typ`` beside the new layouts. A lone copied layout that imports a missing theme produces a Typst error. Guidedog never overwrites an existing customized template during a build."
msgstr ""

#: ../../templates.rst:409
msgid "Like HTML templates, Typst templates are not restricted to a single file. We can split book styling, macros, and custom covers into several ``.typ`` files under ``_templates/``. Standard Typst ``#import`` and ``#include`` statements put them together:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../templates.rst:414
msgid "_templates/book.typ"
msgstr ""

#: ../../templates.rst:436
msgid "Here ``apply-styles(body)`` must apply its rules around the content it receives. A helper that sets text and returns nothing cannot change its caller's later body. The complete tutorial shows a working wrapper around the generated book template."
msgstr ""

#: ../../templates.rst:440
msgid "Page breaks in captioned content"
msgstr ""

#: ../../templates.rst:443
msgid "Guidedog's shared Typst preamble lets captioned tables and code listings continue across pages. Table headers repeat on each table page. This also applies to existing copies of the book template and to standalone PDF conversion."
msgstr ""

#: ../../templates.rst:445
msgid "When we customize their layout, a breakable table or code block must remain inside breakable containers. An unbreakable enclosing figure can otherwise force its contents past the page boundary. Image figures keep their existing layout behavior. :doc:`evidence/pdf-pagination` records the root cause and regression checks."
msgstr ""

